reports/.discarded/
reports/_stale_*

# --- pre-seeded from career-ops v1.27.0 updater (see note) ---
# config/local-paths.txt is deliberately TRACKED in this fork, not ignored.
# It declares pdf-config.mjs, which upstream does not ship. While it was
# ignored, CI checked out the repo without it, every declared path read as an
# orphan there, and validate-system-paths-coverage.mjs was red on all three
# platforms while passing locally — the exact loop upstream #2991 describes.
# v1.31.0 excludes the declaration file from the coverage manifest so a fork
# can commit it; do not re-add an ignore rule for it.
career-dashboard.exe
!web/package-lock.json
.playwright-mcp/
test-results/
playwright-report/
plan/
.career-ops-web/
!test-fixtures/**
documents/*
!documents/.gitkeep
!documents/README.md
data/intake-state.json
data/agent-inbox.md

# fork-check.mjs — detects fork-local work that an update is about to destroy.
# Same preservation mechanism as redline-check.mjs: untracked + absent from
# SYSTEM_PATHS is the entire reason `apply` leaves it alone. Its highest-value
# check derives the prune-exposed set mechanically (tracked ∩ allowlisted dir
# prefix ∩ absent from upstream ∩ not in apply()'s preserved set), which is the
# question a hand-maintained inventory keeps getting wrong.
fork-check.mjs

# Added by career-ops update-system.mjs.
# System-owned ignore rules that were missing from this file. Your own rules
# are never modified, reordered or removed: the updater only appends patterns
# it cannot already find somewhere in this file. Reordering these lines, or
# moving them elsewhere in the file, is safe and will not bring them back.
# Deleting or commenting one out is not: they are system-owned, several of
# them guard files holding personal data, and the next update re-adds any
# that is no longer present as a live pattern.
# The tracker's derived SQLite index (tracker.mjs, #918). It sits beside the
# tracker markdown, so on the standard layout it lands in data/ and is already
# covered by the rules above — but resolveTrackerPath() falls back to
# `<root>/applications.md` when data/applications.md is absent, and the index
# follows it there. That is the legacy install layout, and it is also every
# fresh clone: `node test-all.mjs` leaves a 36KB applications.db in the repo
# root, which `git add .` then stages.
# Its content is the whole tracker — company, role, score, status, notes — so it
# carries the same PII as the file it indexes, under a name none of the rules
# above match. SQLite's -wal/-shm sidecars travel with it.
# Deliberately not root-anchored. The index follows the tracker markdown, and
# getCareerOpsRoot() resolves a relative CAREER_OPS_ROOT (or .career-ops-data
# marker) against the codebase directory — so a data root configured as
# `career-data` puts both inside the checkout, in neither of the two places an
# anchored rule would cover. Nothing tracked in this repo ends in .db, and an
# ignore rule cannot untrack a file that is already tracked, so the cost of the
# wider glob is a `git add -f` on a .db fixture nobody has added yet; the cost
# of the narrower one is this leak, for every non-default data root.

